All green. I built this slice as a single stdlib-only file and ran the full test suite in the sandbox against canned RSS/arXiv/YouTube feeds plus a real git repo — **11/11 checks passed**, including three consecutive end-to-end runs proving incremental dedupe (4 drops → 0 re-drops → exactly 1 new paper), the two-commit git review trail, locking, and dry-run.

One clarification before the code: the Postgres sync engine doesn't exist yet — that's the *next* slice. Today, "ripple into the graph" means the Librarian edits markdown and Obsidian's graph updates live. So this agent is built with the seam already in place: a `sync.command` hook that fires after every successful Librarian run, plus JSON run reports. When we build the projection engine, it plugs in with one config line — zero changes to this agent.

