## Design decisions worth knowing

- **Hash incrementality at two levels:** file sha256 decides upserts; chunk sha256 decides embedding calls. Editing one paragraph re-embeds one chunk, not the note — and a no-op sync costs zero API calls (the self-test asserts both).
- **Redirect stubs are resolved, not indexed:** links through merged entities retarget to the canonical node at parse time; stubs never appear in search or graph results.
- **Embedder identity is guarded:** provider/model/dim live in `meta`; switching hash→OpenAI without `--full` refuses to run, so you can never silently mix vector spaces.
- **The hash embedder is lexical, not semantic** — it exists so the pipeline runs with zero keys. Production quality comes from `text-embedding-3-small`; the fusion code is identical either way.
- **Known limitation (deliberate):** edges are rebuilt only for changed source files. If a redirect stub's target changes, run a weekly `--full` (cheap: it re-embeds nothing whose chunks match). Typed edges beyond MENTIONS/REFERENCES/RELATED_TO come with the UI slice.
- **What the self-test covers:** parser (zones, aliases, redirects, dangling links, edge typing), chunker, embedder determinism, four consecutive syncs (index → no-op → incremental edit with chunk reuse → delete), search ranking/filter/expansion, and all four HTTP endpoints live. What it can't cover on your behalf: the PgStore SQL against a real server — the `--init-db` + first `--full` run is your 2-minute smoke test, and I've kept every query simple and parameterized for exactly that reason.

